-- title:
--   Ideal cutting out the theta equation for a fixed matrix
-- statement:
--   Fix natural numbers $g, N, n$, a commutative ring $S$ (in the base universe), and $X$ a framed polarised abelian scheme of type $(g, N+1, n)$ over $S$: thus $X$ carries an abelian scheme $X.f : X.A \to \operatorname{Spec} S$ with commutative relative group law $X.L$, level structure and invertible polarisation module $X.\mathrm{pol}$, together with a frame, that is a projective presentation $X.\mathrm{frame}$ of $X.\mathrm{pol}$ relative to $X.f$ with $N+1$ global sections whose associated morphism $X.\mathrm{frame}.\mathrm{toProj} : X.A \to \mathbb{P}^N_S$ is a closed immersion and whose sections form a section basis on the whole of $X.A$. Let $U$ be an invertible $(N+1)\times(N+1)$ matrix over $S$ and let $x_0$ be a section of $X.f$ over the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} S$, i.e. an $S$-point of $X.A$. The assertion is that there exists a finitely generated ideal $J \subseteq S$ with the following property. Let $T$ be a commutative ring, $\varphi : S \to T$ a ring homomorphism, and $Y$ a framed polarised abelian scheme of type $(g, N+1, n)$ over $T$ which is a pullback of $X$ along $\varphi$, in the sense that there is a morphism $g_A : Y.A \to X.A$ making $Y.A$ the fibre product of $X.A$ with $\operatorname{Spec} T$ over $\operatorname{Spec} S$, compatible with the two group laws on points over any base, carrying the marked $n$-torsion sections of $Y$ to the base changes of those of $X$, with $g_A^{*} X.\mathrm{pol} \cong Y.\mathrm{pol}$, and such that $Y.\mathrm{frame}.\mathrm{toProj}$ followed by $\mathbb{P}^N_T \to \mathbb{P}^N_S$ equals $g_A$ followed by $X.\mathrm{frame}.\mathrm{toProj}$. Let $P'$ be any projective presentation of $Y.\mathrm{pol}$ relative to $Y.f$ with $N+1$ sections whose sections are obtained from those of $Y.\mathrm{frame}$ by the matrix $\varphi(U)$, namely $P'.\sigma_i = \sum_j \varphi(U_{ij}) \cdot Y.\mathrm{frame}.\sigma_j$, the scalars being the images of $\varphi(U_{ij})$ in $\Gamma(Y.A, \top)$ under the structure morphism. Let finally $x$ be a $T$-point of $Y.f$ which is the base change of $x_0$ in the sense that $x$ followed by $Y.\mathrm{frame}.\mathrm{toProj}$ followed by $\mathbb{P}^N_T \to \mathbb{P}^N_S$ equals $\operatorname{Spec} \varphi$ followed by $x_0$ followed by $X.\mathrm{frame}.\mathrm{toProj}$ (with $T$ viewed as an $S$-algebra via $\varphi$). Then the translation by $x$ on $Y.A \times_{\operatorname{Spec} T} \operatorname{Spec} T$, composed with the first projection and $Y.\mathrm{frame}.\mathrm{toProj}$, equals the first projection followed by $P'.\mathrm{toProj}$ if and only if the ideal of $T$ generated by $\varphi(J)$ is zero.
--
--   This is the Grothendieck-style "scheme of coincidences" statement for the theta equation attached to a single matrix $U$: the locus where translation by the base change of a given $S$-point matches the reframing of the projective embedding by $U$ is cut out, after arbitrary base change, by one finitely generated ideal. It feeds the variant in which the translating point is only required to exist, [`AlgebraicGeometry.FramedPolarisedAbelianScheme.exists_ideal_fg_forall_isPullback_exists_translate_comp_eq_iff_map_eq_bot`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.FramedPolarisedAbelianScheme.exists_ideal_fg_forall_isPullback_exists_translate_comp_eq_iff_map_eq_bot).
